  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Considerable showers continue with mountain snow above 6,500 \n   feet late tonight into early Monday, mainly through central \n   Idaho and northwest Montana.\n\n-  Drying and warming conditions by mid-week.\n\n-  Trending wetter late Thursday into Friday.\n\nAn upper low moving through eastern Washington will track into \nnorthwest Montana tonight, continuing to bring periodic showers, \nisolated thunderstorms, and gusty southwest winds across much of \nthe Northern Rockies.\n\nThe heaviest precipitation will target north-central Idaho and \nnorthwest Montana, where mountain locations could receive up to an\ninch of total precipitation, with lighter amounts in the valleys.\nSnow levels will fall near 6,500 feet tonight, bringing light \nsnow accumulations along the Continental Divide southward to the \nBitterroots. These cold, raw conditions will create a heightened \nhypothermia risk for backcountry recreationists. Farther south and\neast, including Missoula, rainfall will remain light and spotty. \nIn contrast, southwest Montana will stay mostly dry with strong, \ngusty winds, driving critical fire weather conditions through this\nevening (Red Flag Warning).\n\nThe system exits by Tuesday morning, clearing the way for a \nbriefly warmer and drier pattern through mid-week, though shower \nchances linger near the Canadian border. Clearing skies early \nTuesday will bring chilly temperatures to areas along and south of\nI-90. Sub- freezing temperatures are anticipated in the higher \nvalleys of southwest Montana and along the Divide, while frost \nwill likely impact the typical colder locations in the Missoula, \nBitterroot, and Clearwater valleys.\n\nA trend in the ensemble guidance points toward another upper- \nlevel low cutting off along the Pacific coast Wednesday night \nbefore drifting across western Montana and north-central Idaho \nThursday into Friday. A monsoonal moisture tap appears to be \ninteracting with the low causing precipitation chances to trend \nupward late in the week. Approximately 60 percent of ensemble \nsolutions support this wetter scenario, though overall confidence \nin exact timing and local details remain low.",
